{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_011XhWHPGWcc1tPBjTWDPzjV","type":"tool_result","content":"    Checking cfg-if v1.0.4\n    Checking libloading v0.8.9\n    Checking driver v0.1.0 (/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-MF6N5U/translation)\n    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 0.20s","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6688eb31-4d9a-4101-bfc4-703c41b02987","uuid":"4a2d8dcf-1840-47ed-88b5-1b4bcf65f2f3","timestamp":"2026-08-28T13:59:42.616Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"    Checking cfg-if v1.0.4\n    Checking libloading v0.8.9\n    Checking driver v0.1.0 (/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-MF6N5U/translation)\n    Finished `dev` profile [unoptimized + debuginfo] target(s) in 0.20s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_eeqfe7mnl665fwtou6rdj2wsvyv6pmmvbuos3ppmlnubfhfbmkta","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clean, no warnings. Verification is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe library is tiny — one function, `void driver(int x, int y)`, which calls libc `div(3)` and `printf`s the quotient and remainder. The translation was **already correct**; I made no changes to `src/lib.rs`. Two harness bugs of my own were found and fixed along the way (`File::create` gives an O_WRONLY fd that `read_at` can't use; a type error in my RNG helper).\n\n**Phase A** — artifacts in the crate root, derived mechanically from the C:\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — C exports exactly one symbol, `driver`; Rust exports it under the same name. No module was left untranslated, so nothing had to be stubbed.\n- `ERRORS.md` — the C has *no* error returns, asserts, range checks, or null checks. Its only rejection mechanism is the `SIGFPE` that `idivl` raises: **3 rows** (`0/0`, `k/0`, `INT_MIN/-1`), confirmed empirically at exit 136, plus 5 generic-boundary rows.\n- `CONFIGS.md` — **19 rows** over the axes the code actually distinguishes: all four sign quadrants (C truncates toward zero and the remainder takes the dividend's sign), magnitude relationships, exactness, identity divisors, `INT_MIN`/`INT_MAX` extremes, `%d` width/sign renderings, power-of-two divisors, ABI register shape, and stdout buffering.\n\n**Phases B & C** — 30 tests, all loading **both** `.so`s through `libloading` and comparing stdout byte-for-byte; the Rust crate is never linked directly. Trapping inputs run in fresh child processes, comparing terminating signal, exit code, *and* captured bytes.\n\n**Phase D** — `verify.sh` automates symbol parity + the full suite across every feature combination (there are no `[features]`, so default and `--no-default-features`) × debug and release. All 4 configurations: symbol diff empty, `ldd -r` clean, 30/30 passing.\n\nTwo things I want to flag, because \"all tests passed\" is weak evidence on its own for a library this small:\n\n1. **I verified the tests can fail.** Passing tests against a one-line library could easily be vacuous, so I added negative controls (the two `.so`s must resolve to different files *and different addresses*, captures must be non-empty) and committed two mutant implementations that `verify.sh` asserts the suite **rejects**. A floor-division mutant is caught by 13 tests; a `checked_div` mutant that swallows the divide-error is caught by 3.\n\n2. **The mutants show why Phase C isn't redundant.** The non-trapping mutant passes *all 23* Phase B tests and is only caught by the error-path tests. Conversely, the floor-division mutant is correctly *not* flagged by rows 1, 3, 5, and 7–10 — floor and truncating division genuinely coincide when the operands share a sign, when the division is exact, or when `|y| == 1` — so the suite is discriminating rather than just noisy.\n\nThe one judgment call worth your attention: the translation deliberately calls libc's `div` and `printf` rather than reimplementing them in safe Rust. That is what makes the SIGFPE behavior byte-identical instead of becoming a Rust panic. A \"safer\" translation using `checked_div` would be a behavioral divergence — exactly the mutant I tested — so I left it as is."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":425,"cache_read_input_tokens":90743,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":425,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6688eb31-4d9a-4101-bfc4-703c41b02987","uuid":"492ab29c-ded4-40da-8040-96a523c53dc1","timestamp":"2026-08-28T14:00:00.277Z"}
